How Gmail SMTP works                                                                                                                                 
                                                                                           
  Gmail lets you use their mail servers to send emails from your own code. Normally Google blocks this for security, but they allow it through App 
  Passwords — a special 16-character password generated just for one app.                                                                              
   
  ---                                                                                                                                                  
  Step-by-step setup                                        

  1. Enable 2-Step Verification on your Google account
  - Go to https://myaccount.google.com → Security
  - Turn on 2-Step Verification if it's off (required before App Passwords work)

  2. Generate an App Password
  - Go to: Google Account → Security → App passwords (search for it if you don't see it)
  - Select app: Mail, Select device: Other → type "Intervexa" → click Generate
  - Google gives you a 16-character password like abcd efgh ijkl mnop — copy it

  3. Add to backend/.env
  SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
  SMTP_PORT=587
  SMTP_SECURE=false
  SMTP_USER=your.email@gmail.com
  SMTP_PASS=abcdefghijklmnop
  SMTP_FROM="Intervexa" <your.email@gmail.com>
  FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:3000
  ▎ Remove spaces from the app password. Use the same Gmail account for both SMTP_USER and SMTP_FROM.

  4. Restart the backend
  cd backend && npm run dev

  ---
  What happens then

  When a user registers → your backend calls Gmail's SMTP server on port 587 → Gmail sends the email from your address → user receives it in their
  inbox with a verify link pointing to http://localhost:3000/verify-email?token=....

  ---
  Limitations to know

  - Gmail free tier allows ~500 emails/day — more than enough for a FYP demo
  - The verification link uses localhost:3000, so whoever clicks it must open it on the same machine running the frontend. For a demo to others, set
  FRONTEND_URL to your deployed/ngrok URL instead.